  Under A the persistence test has to mutate [38;5;153mglobalThis[39m and clean up after itself; under C it's a plain object passed
  in. That's the difference between a test suite that stays honest as it grows and one that develops spooky ordering
  failures.

  [1mWhy not B:[0m the string-coercion tax is real and recurring, and its one advantage — isolated corruption — matters little
  when there are three preferences and a corrupt read just means falling back to defaults anyway.

  Two things I'd fold into C regardless:

  - [1mDual export.[0m A short footer — [38;5;153mmodule.exports[39m when [38;5;153mmodule[39m is defined, otherwise assign to [38;5;153mwindow.Preferences[39m — so the
    same file serves the [38;5;153m<script>[39m tag and [38;5;153mrequire()[39m. No build step needed.
  - [1mStorage unavailable is not a crash.[0m Safari private mode throws on [38;5;153msetItem[39m, and the page can be opened over [38;5;153mfile://[39m.
    The module catches, falls back to an in-memory store, and the app keeps working with preferences that simply don't
    survive the session.

  One thing the answers don't settle, which I'll propose in the design rather than ask about: [1mthe page has no CSS at [0m
  [1mall[0m, not even inline. A theme toggle needs something to toggle. I'd add a small [38;5;153mstyles.css[39m with a [38;5;153m[data-theme="dark"][39m
  block and have the module set [38;5;153mdata-theme[39m on [38;5;153m<html>[39m — minimal, and it keeps theming out of JS.

  Blind spot worth naming: because Codex returned nothing, these three approaches are single-source. If there's a fourth
  shape none of us considered, this gate didn't catch it.
